Dates, Time and Internationalisation:
The constructor has three unrelated meanings, and that asymmetry is where day-off bugs come from:

```js
console.log(new Date('2024-01-01').toISOString());        // date-only string is UTC
console.log(new Date(2024, 0, 1).toISOString());          // numeric form is LOCAL
console.log(new Date(Date.UTC(2024, 0, 1)).toISOString());
console.log(new Date('2024-01-01T00:00').toISOString());  // no offset -> local
```

Always check validity, because an invalid date does not throw:

```js
const d = new Date('not a date');
console.log(d.toString(), d.getTime());     // 'Invalid Date' NaN
console.log(Number.isNaN(d.getTime()));     // true — the check that matters
const parsed = new Date('2024-02-30');
console.log(parsed.toISOString());          // 2024-03-01 — overflow is normalised
```

Arithmetic and formatting, done the ways that handle time zones:

```js
const now = new Date('2024-03-31T12:00:00Z');
const plusOneDay = new Date(now.getTime() + 86_400_000);
console.log(plusOneDay.toISOString());

const fmt = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { dateStyle: 'full', timeStyle: 'short', timeZone: 'UTC' });
console.log(fmt.format(now));
console.log(Intl.RelativeTimeFormat('en', { numeric: 'auto' }).format(-1, 'day'));   // yesterday
```

Calendar arithmetic must go through the fields, not milliseconds, because a day is not always 24 hours:

```js
const addDays = (date, days) => {
  const next = new Date(date);
  next.setDate(next.getDate() + days);     // survives a DST change
  return next;
};
console.log(addDays(new Date('2024-03-01T00:00:00Z'), 31).toISOString());
```

Debugging and Developer Tools:
Console methods that format instead of dump:

```js
const rows = [{ id: 1, name: 'Ana' }, { id: 2, name: 'Bo' }];
console.table(rows);
console.time('loop');
for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {}
console.timeEnd('loop');
console.group('request');
console.log('id', 7);
console.groupEnd();
console.assert(1 === 2, 'this prints only when false');
console.dir({ nested: { deep: true } }, { depth: null, colors: true });
```

Stack inspection and error context:

```js
function inner() { console.trace('from here'); }
function outer() { inner(); }
outer();

try {
  throw new Error('root cause');
} catch (err) {
  const wrapped = new Error('while saving', { cause: err });
  console.error(wrapped.message, '->', wrapped.cause.message);
  console.error(wrapped.stack.split('\n').slice(0, 3).join('\n'));
}
```

A breakpoint beats a log line for control flow, and it needs no redeploy:

```js
function total(items) {
  debugger;                       // pauses when devtools is open
  return items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.qty * i.price, 0);
}
```

In Node the same thing is available over the inspector: `node --inspect-brk app.js` then attach a client. Add `--enable-source-maps` so the stack points at your TypeScript instead of the bundle, and group production errors by fingerprint — message plus top frame — so one bug does not create a thousand report items.

Common Pitfalls and Idioms:
`forEach` does not await, `sort` is lexicographic, and both look right:

```js
// WRONG: the loop returns before any promise resolves
[1, 2, 3].forEach(async (n) => { await save(n); });
console.log('this runs first');

// RIGHT
for (const n of [1, 2, 3]) await save(n);
await Promise.all([1, 2, 3].map(save));

// WRONG: string comparison
console.log([10, 9, 80].sort());              // [10, 80, 9]
// RIGHT
console.log([10, 9, 80].sort((a, b) => a - b));   // [9, 10, 80]
```

`this` is lost through a callback, and `NaN` is not equal to itself:

```js
const timer = {
  seconds: 0,
  startBroken() { setInterval(function () { this.seconds++; }, 1000); },
  startFixed() { setInterval(() => { this.seconds++; }, 1000); },
};

const x = NaN;
console.log(x === x);                 // false
console.log(Number.isNaN(x));         // true
console.log([1, NaN, 2].indexOf(NaN));      // -1
console.log([1, NaN, 2].includes(NaN));     // true
```

Immutability mistakes and the reference-comparison trap:

```js
const a = { list: [1, 2] };
const b = { ...a };            // shallow copy
b.list.push(3);
console.log(a.list);           // [1, 2, 3] — same nested array

// WRONG: distinct objects are never equal
// if (obj === { id: 1 }) { }
// RIGHT
const same = (x, y) => x?.id === y.id;

// WRONG: JSON round-trip loses Date, Map, Set, undefined and Infinity
// RIGHT: structuredClone
const original = { when: new Date(), tags: new Set(['a']) };
const clone = structuredClone(original);
console.log(clone.when instanceof Date, clone.tags instanceof Set);   // true true
```

Glossary and Cheat Sheets:
Operator precedence, in the order that actually matters when reading code:

```js
// member . [] ()  then unary ! typeof ++ --  then **  then * / %
// then + -  then < > <= >= in instanceof  then == != === !==
// then &&  then || ??  then ?:  then = += ...  then ,
console.log(2 + 3 * 4);          // 14
console.log((2 + 3) * 4);        // 20
console.log(2 ** 3 ** 2);        // 512 — right-associative
console.log(!0 + 1);             // 2 — ! binds tighter than +
console.log(1 < 2 < 3);          // true  ((1<2) -> true -> 1 < 3)
console.log(3 > 2 > 1);          // false ((3>2) -> true -> 1 > 1)
```

Falsy values, equality, and the one loose comparison worth keeping:

```js
// falsy: false 0 -0 0n '' null undefined NaN
typeof undefined        // 'undefined'
typeof null             // 'object'  (the historical bug)
Object.is(NaN, NaN)     // true
Object.is(0, -0)        // false
value == null           // true for both null and undefined, false for 0 and ''
```

Array and object recipes worth having at hand:

```js
